{"id":1098,"date":"2020-11-20T12:23:53","date_gmt":"2020-11-20T20:23:53","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1098"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:22:05","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:22:05","slug":"codrone-arduino-flight-controller","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-flight-controller\/","title":{"rendered":"Flight Controller with Analog Read"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab696\" name=\"6a2215caab696\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Analog joysticks<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Your CoDrone controller uses two analog joysticks to control your CoDrone&#8217;s roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. One thing: you still need to program the joysticks. Luckily there are only three steps to develop your controller&#8217;s program:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read the joysticks&#8217; values<\/li>\n<li>Convert the joysticks&#8217; values to input values (-100 to 100)<\/li>\n<li>Send the converted values to the CoDrone<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__step-media\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1099&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab810\" name=\"6a2215caab810\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>analogRead()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Remember programming your IR sensors? To read the sensors&#8217; values, you used <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalRead({your pin number here})<\/code>. You&#8217;re going to use a similar command for your joysticks! That command will look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">analogRead({your pin number here});<\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">analogRead<\/code>reads the sensor value with a range from 0 to 1023.<\/li>\n<li>The pin number specifies which sensor you want to be read.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab8ae\" name=\"6a2215caab8ae\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Analog vs digital<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>But wait! Why would you use analog? Isn&#8217;t that what old technology uses? Not quite. The big difference between analog and digital is that analog can have range of infinite values, while digital signals can be only 1 (true) or 0 (false)\u2014like a switch that only turns on and off. Analog signals are better for things you might need more detail with. Here are some examples!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Digital examples<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Digital IR sensors: Some IR sensors, like the ones you have on the bottom of your CoDrone remote control, are used for digital sensing. These sensors work like switches and let you know if they are either covered or uncovered.<\/li>\n<li>Buttons: They&#8217;re digital too! They&#8217;re either pressed or not pressed.<\/li>\n<li>Touch sensors: These are very similar to buttons. They&#8217;re either touched or not touched.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Analog examples<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Joysticks: They&#8217;re analog because they let you know not only if the joystick is pressed, but also how far it&#8217;s pressed.<\/li>\n<li>Analog IR sensors: These tell you not only if they are covered or not, but also the distance between the sensor and what is covering it. You have three on your CoDrone&#8217;s controller.<\/li>\n<li>Thermometers: Instead of telling you if something is hot or not, it tells you the exact temperature.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab93b\" name=\"6a2215caab93b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using analogRead()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Back to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">analogRead()<\/code>! You&#8217;re going to use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">analogRead()<\/code>\u00a0to read your joysticks&#8217; values. If you used the Building the Remote lesson\u00a0to wire your remote control, your joysticks should be connected to pins 22 through 25. Check our\u00a0<a href=\"http:\/\/www.robolink.com\/codrone-documentation\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-link-type=\"LINK\">Smart Inventor Pin-Out Guide<\/a>\u00a0if you&#8217;re still not sure!<\/p>\n<p>Your code to read all of your joystick values would look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  int yaw = analogRead(22);\r\n  int throttle = analogRead(23);\r\n  int roll = analogRead(24);\r\n  int pitch = analogRead(25);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Begin communication at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Set the yaw variable to read and get the value for pin 22.<\/li>\n<li>Set the throttle variable to read\u00a0and get the value for\u00a0pin 23.<\/li>\n<li>Set the roll variable to read\u00a0and get the value for\u00a0pin 24.<\/li>\n<li>Set the pitch variable to read\u00a0and get the value for\u00a0pin 25.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Each of your joysticks has two sets of wires coming out of it, making four inputs. One set reads the left and right movement of your joystick, while the other set reads the up and down movement.<\/p>\n<p>For an example of what your joysticks are seeing, look at the throttle joystick, which is connected to pin 23. Since analog values range from 0 to 1023, pushing your throttle joystick all the way up will save its value as 1023, while pushing it down will save the value as 0. Leaving the throttle joystick in the middle would save its value at about 512, which is around halfway between 0 and 1023.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1100&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1101&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab994\" name=\"6a2215caab994\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Scale change<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Right now you have your roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle variables reading and getting the values for their pins, but those values are going to be between 0 and 1023. For your CoDrone&#8217;s remote control, they need to be translated to a range of -100 to 100. Luckily, this doesn&#8217;t take giant math equations &#8212; just a scale change!<\/p>\n<p>Wait, what?<\/p>\n<p>Basically, you need to use the values from one scale and re-map them to another scale. In the case of your joysticks&#8217; values, you would need to translate them from the 0 &#8211; 1023 scale to the -100 &#8211; 100 scale. 0 would turn into -100 since they&#8217;re both the lowest values, 1023 would turn into 100 since they&#8217;re both the highest values, and 512 would be 0 since they&#8217;re both in the middle. The scale change would look a lot like the picture below.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1102&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caab9fd\" name=\"6a2215caab9fd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The only problem is that you can&#8217;t just input new values in Arduino and hope it turns out well. What you can do instead is use the analog scale change function:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange()<\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li>What goes between the parentheses is the number that you want to be remapped from the 0 &#8211; 1023 scale to the -100 &#8211; 100 scale.<\/li>\n<li>Once this function is used, the values that are returned will be from -100 to 100.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The easiest way to use this function is to combine it with the variables you wrote earlier. For example, using it with your yaw variable would look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setYaw(CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22)));<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caaba5c\" name=\"6a2215caaba5c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Correcting for direction<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your joysticks&#8217; horizontal motion works somewhat differently than their vertical motion. That range is from 1023 to 0, instead of the other way around. Look at the pictures below to help you out! Since movement to the left is associated with negative numbers, you will need to multiply the lines for yaw and throttle by -1. This will convert the analog values correctly.<\/p>\n<p>This is what it will look like in your code:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<div class=\"cpy-clip-wrap u-hide-print\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setYaw( -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22)) );    \r\nCoDrone.setThrottle( CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(23)) );   \r\nCoDrone.setRoll( -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(24)) ); \r\nCoDrone.setPitch( CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(25)) );<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1103&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caabac4\" name=\"6a2215caabac4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your final code should look something like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n    CoDrone.pair();\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n  byte bt4 = digitalRead(14);\r\n  byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n\r\n  if (PAIRING == true){  \r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setYaw( -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22)) );    \r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle( CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(23)) );   \r\n    CoDrone.setRoll( -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(24)) ); \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch( CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(25)) ); \r\n    CoDrone.move(ROLL, PITCH, YAW, THROTTLE);   \r\n\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  if (bt1 &amp;&amp; !bt4 &amp;&amp; !bt8){\r\n    CoDrone.emergencyStop();\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">if (PAIRING == true)<\/code> only executes when the CoDrone is paired with the remote control and will prevent commands from being sent when the CoDrone disconnects. You don&#8217;t have to include this in your code, but it&#8217;s a really good idea!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215caabb30\" name=\"6a2215caabb30\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p><strong>Challenge: In control<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create and upload your own controller code!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>You have to include a kill switch!<\/li>\n<li>Include an extra maneuver switch. For example, if you cover the left sensor, your CoDrone will yaw to the right for 2 seconds, then yaw back to the left for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Analog joysticks [vc_column_text] Your CoDrone controller uses two analog joysticks to control your CoDrone's roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. One thing: you still need to program the&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-flight-controller\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1751,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1438,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2193,2163,2135,2164,2165,2122,2111],"class_list":["post-1098","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-analog","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-remote-control","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1098","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1098\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1104,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1098\/revisions\/1104"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1751"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1098"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1098"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1098"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}